==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[incumber]] {{i|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|encombren}}, from {{der|en|fro|encombrer}}, from {{m|fro|en-}} + {{m|fro|combrer||to hinder}}; see {{m|en|cumber}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈkʌmbə(ɹ)/|/ɛnˈkʌmbə(ɹ)/}}
* {{rhyme|en|ʌmbə(ɹ)|s=3}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-encumber.wav|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[load]] down something with a [[burden]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=November|author=K. Westcott Jones|title=Parkeston Quay and the Antwerp Continental Service|journal=Railway Magazine|page=760|text=In the reverse direction, creature comforts were not closely studied, and the vessel left Antwerp at 1 p.m., to arrive in Harwich at the awkward hour of 2 a.m. The boat train was waiting for the weary stream of passengers, who straggled up George Street, '''encumbered''' with luggage, or following burdened porters in the darkness.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[restrict]] or [[block]] something with a [[hindrance]] or [[impediment]].
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=1906 – 1921
|author=w:John Galsworthy
|title=w:The Forsyte Saga
|volume=1
|chapter=Encounter
|passage=He [Timothy Forsyte] had never committed the imprudence of marrying or '''encumbering''' himself in any way with children.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To burden with a [[legal]] [[claim]] or other [[obligation]].

